  "OverallSummary": "This case involves Richard Mireles, who failed to properly supervise a registered representative who excessively traded in the accounts of five customers between July 2020 and December 2022. The excessive trading violated Regulation BI's Care Obligation under Exchange Act Rule 15l-1(a)(2)(ii) and FINRA Rule 2111. Multiple red flags were escalated to Mireles by two different supervisors who identified concerns about high volumes of solicited trades, including in accounts of senior customers. Instead of addressing potential excessive trading, Mireles directed supervisors to only perform trade-by-trade assessments rather than reviewing the pattern of transactions within accounts. As a result, the registered representative's excessive trading continued unchecked, resulting in approximately $2.2 million in trading costs and $2.2 million in realized losses across the five affected customer accounts. Two specifically identified affected customers included a 77-year-old retiree with a capital preservation objective (20.3% cost-to-equity ratio, $490,000+ in trading costs, $550,000+ in losses) and an 88-year-old retiree's trust account with growth and income objective (23.4% cost-to-equity ratio, $650,000+ in trading costs, $560,000+ in losses). For his failure to reasonably supervise, Mireles was suspended for four months in all principal capacities and fined $5,000."
